# A guard that is green locally and blind in CI, and a race the test only hoped to win

Three 2026-10-03 follow-ups on the same push. The first is `scripts/ci/check-load-bearing-features.py`
reporting a lost feature on the runner that it could not see locally; the second is a
`xai-grok-shell` session-title test that passes when the scheduler is on its side and burns eight
seconds when it is not. Both are cases where "the fixture passed" and "the fixture checked anything"
were different questions. The third came out of writing this file: the gate sweep quoted in section
13 had to be produced by a scratch script, so the committed table cited a command no reader can
re-run, and `scripts/verify-gates.sh` is what replaces it.

== 1. What CI actually said

Head `b93a741c`, run `37114697856`. The failing step is not the Windows leg:

    rust check / clippy / test :: load-bearing dependency features
    platform tests (${{ matrix.os }})                       skipped

The whole platform matrix was skipped because the legs depend on that job. That is the second
consecutive push on which the Windows leg never ran, and in both cases the blocker was the Linux
job in front of it: `37110356858` by the session-title race in section 7, `37114697856` by this
guard. The three runs before those did run the matrix (`success,failure`, macOS green and Windows
red), which is where the five-cluster failure analysis came from. The step's own fixture run ended:

    FAIL: test_the_shipped_tree_satisfies_the_committed_table
    AssertionError: Lists differ: ['`serde_json`/`preserve_order` is enabled for
    `xai-grok-tools` on none of x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu, x86_64-pc-windows-msvc,
    aarch64-apple-darwin; the row is stale or the feature was lost ...'] != []

Locally, same commit, same `Cargo.toml`, same table:

    $ git show HEAD:crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/Cargo.toml | grep -n serde_json
    61:serde_json = { workspace = true, features = ["preserve_order"] }
    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-load-bearing-features.py
    check-load-bearing-features: 1 load-bearing feature row(s) hold on every target they name

== 2. The mechanism: cargo colourises on an inherited variable

The CI step env, from the run log:

      CARGO_TERM_COLOR: always
      CARGO_INCREMENTAL: 0
      CARGO_HOME: /home/runner/.cargo

`CARGO_TERM_COLOR` is exported at job level, so every step inherits it, and `cargo tree` obeys it
even with its stdout pointed at a pipe. Capturing the real bytes:

    $ CARGO_TERM_COLOR=always cargo tree -p xai-grok-tools -e features --locked \
          --target x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu -i serde_json | grep -a preserve_order | head -3
    '^[[2m^[[35mM-bM-^TM-^B^[[0m   ^[[2m^[[35mM-bM-^TM-^TM-bM-^TM-^@M-bM-^TM-^@^[[0m serde_json feature "preserve_order"'
    '^[[2m^[[35mM-bM-^TM-^\M-bM-^TM-^@M-bM-^TM-^@^[[0m serde_json feature "preserve_order" ^[[33m^[[2m(*)^[[39m^[[22m'

The escapes sit in front of the tree glyphs and wrap the `(*)` repeat marker. The parser stripped
glyph characters with `lstrip("│├└─ ")`, so a label beginning with `\x1b` matched neither the
package regex nor the feature regex.

== 3. Why that produced "present, but featureless" rather than "dependency absent"

    $ CARGO_TERM_COLOR=always cargo tree ... | sed -n '1,3p' | cat -v
    serde_json v1.0.149
    ^[[2m^[[35mM-bM-^TM-^\M-bM-^TM-^@M-bM-^TM-^@^[[0m serde_json feature "alloc"
    ^[[2m^[[35mM-bM-^TM-^B^[[0m   ^[[2mM-bM-^TM-^TM-bM-^TM-^@M-bM-^TM-^@^[[0m schemars v1.0.4

The root line has no glyphs and no escapes, so it still matched `PACKAGE_NODE` and set `present =
True`. Every feature line failed to match. The one problem the run reported is exactly that
combination: the dependency is there, the feature is nowhere. A reader would have gone looking for
a lost feature in `Cargo.toml`, which is what the message says and what was not true.

This is the same class of miss as the `--edges` question below: a guard reading a tool's stdout has
to pin the tool's output mode, because the ambient environment of the machine running CI is not the
environment of the machine writing the guard.

== 4. The fix, three layers, each pinned by its own fixture

    $ grep -n -A9 'proc = subprocess.run' scripts/ci/check-load-bearing-features.py | head -12
              "cargo", "tree", "-p", crate, "-e", "features", "--locked",
              # Required, not cosmetic: see ANSI_SEQUENCE.
              "--color", "never",
              # A dev-dependency can hand the feature to the test build while the
              # shipped binary still lacks it, and every row in the table is about
              # runtime behaviour. Only the edges `cargo build` resolves count.
              "--edges", "no-dev",
              "--target", target, "-i", dependency,

1. `--color never` on the command line, so the output mode is decided by the caller.
2. `parse_tree` strips `ANSI_SEQUENCE` before matching, so a future call site that forgets the flag
   reads a coloured tree as the same data instead of as "no features".
3. The end-to-end fixture that runs the real graph now runs under a forced
   `CARGO_TERM_COLOR=always`, so a developer shell that leaves colour off cannot hide the
   regression. With layers 1 and 2 both present, removing either one alone still leaves the
   end-to-end green by design; each layer's own unit fixture is what fails when that layer is
   removed. That is stated here so the coverage is not mistaken for redundancy.

== 5. Mutations against the guard

    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-check-load-bearing-features.py
    Ran 30 tests ... OK

Applied one at a time against the working tree, each followed by a byte-for-byte restore.

    A: --color never dropped from the cargo tree argv
      exit=1 Ran 30 tests; FAILED (failures=1)
      failing: test_the_tree_is_requested_without_colour [CommandShape]
    B: ANSI sequences no longer stripped before matching labels
      exit=1 Ran 30 tests; FAILED (failures=2)
      failing: test_a_coloured_capture_yields_the_same_features_as_a_plain_one [TreeParsing],
               test_the_coloured_capture_still_says_nothing_about_features_it_lacks [TreeParsing]
    C: --edges no-dev dropped from the cargo tree argv
      exit=1 Ran 30 tests; FAILED (failures=1)
      failing: test_dev_dependency_edges_are_excluded [CommandShape]

Each mutation is caught by exactly the fixture that names it, and by nothing else. Both files were
restored and compared byte for byte (`RESTORED byte-identical`). Fixture count 25 to 30.

After the fix, in both environments:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-load-bearing-features.py
    check-load-bearing-features: 1 load-bearing feature row(s) hold on every target they name
    $ CARGO_TERM_COLOR=always python3 scripts/ci/check-load-bearing-features.py
    check-load-bearing-features: 1 load-bearing feature row(s) hold on every target they name

== 6. What `--edges no-dev` changed about the row itself

    $ for t in x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu x86_64-pc-windows-msvc aarch64-apple-darwin; do
          printf '%s nodes=' "$t"
          cargo tree -p xai-grok-tools -e features --locked --color never --edges no-dev \
              --target $t -i serde_json | grep -c 'serde_json feature "preserve_order"'
      done
    x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu nodes=3
    x86_64-pc-windows-msvc nodes=3
    aarch64-apple-darwin nodes=3

With dev edges removed the row still holds on all three targets, and the Windows tree now names the
provider explicitly:

    ├── serde_json feature "indexmap"
    │   └── serde_json feature "preserve_order"
    │       └── xai-grok-tools v0.1.220-alpha.4 (/repo/crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools)

Before this batch the feature reached `xai-grok-tools` only through `nono`, a `cfg(unix)` dependency
of `xai-grok-sandbox`. With dev edges in play, a dev-only dependency could have satisfied the row
while `cargo build` still produced a binary without the feature; the rows in the table are about
rendered form fields at runtime, so that would have been a false green of exactly the kind the gate
was written to catch.

== 7. The second item: a CI test failure that only CI saw

Run `37110356858` (head `ba85c87c`), `rust check / clippy / test`:

    6871 passed; 1 failed; 32 ignored; 0 measured; finished in 95.01s
    failures:
        session::persistence::durable_update_tests::
          reset_title_to_auto_adopts_in_flight_generation_as_auto
    ... in-flight GeneratedTitle after unpin never adopted
    ERROR xai_grok_sampler::client: Failed to build HTTP request: builder error

The test's own doc comment states the precondition it then fails to create: "Disk is already blank
when the stale `GeneratedTitle` arrives".

    Mechanism. The first ContentChunk makes SummaryGenerator::update tokio::spawn a title task.
    The default SamplerConfig has base_url = "" and model = "", so reqwest fails to build the
    request and no socket is ever opened; generate_session_summary returns "" and the task falls
    back to title_fallback_from_user_text and sends GeneratedTitle -- essentially immediately.
    If that message reaches the actor before storage::reset_title_to_auto has blanked the disk,
    generated_title_if_absent sees the manual title still there and refuses it (persistence.rs:2015
    logs "skipped auto-generated title" and returns Ok(false)). The blank then lands, and nothing
    ever retries: the session is left untitled and the test spends its full 8 s budget.

    Measured on this machine before changing anything: 600 runs of the single test (200 idle, 400
    while a full `cargo test -p xai-grok-shell --lib -- --test-threads=16` ran concurrently under
    `nice -n 19`) passed every time in 0.04-0.06 s. Inserting a 300 ms yield between
    `send(ContentChunk)` and the unpin made the same test fail 100 % of the time with the CI panic,
    after 8.38 s.

== 8. Why the production code was left alone

`extensions/session_admin.rs::reset_session_title_to_auto`, the only production caller, re-arms a
whole-conversation retitle after blanking (`SessionCommand::TitleRenamed { manual: false }`, or a
watermark write when the session is dormant). So the lost update is recovered in the product.
Changing `generated_title_if_absent` to accept a title that arrived before the blank would freeze
the auto title at turn 1 for every real session, which is a product decision, not a test fix. The
storage-level gap is recorded as an open TODO row instead of being papered over.

== 9. The fixture: a loopback endpoint that holds the request open

    crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/session/persistence_tests.rs

    endpoint_that_holds_the_title_request()
      binds 127.0.0.1:0, accepts, reads 5 bytes of the request line, and keeps the
      connection with no response until the guard releases it.
    title_sampler_config(base_url)
      api_key + base_url + model + ChatCompletions + max_retries: Some(0)

Two details are load-bearing and are commented as such in the source:

- The endpoint only treats a connection as the title request if the request line starts with
  `POST `. `SamplerClient::post` calls `prewarm::note_first_sampling_use`, which dials the origin
  with `client.get(origin)` before the first real request; taking that connection for the title
  request would put the barrier in the wrong place.
- `base_url` alone would not have been enough. The empty `model` and missing credentials are what
  turn the request into a builder error before any socket is opened, which is precisely why the
  default config cannot express "still in flight". `max_retries: Some(0)` keeps the abort from
  being re-dialled onto a fresh connection.

The test now: sends the chunk, waits for the endpoint to have the request, blanks disk, sends
`ResetTitleToAuto`, `flush_ack`, asserts disk is blank, and only then releases the socket. The
adoption it then waits for is the one the test is named for.

== 10. Mutations against the title test

Each mutation was injected into `persistence_tests.rs`, the single test run through the real
binary, and the file restored before the next one:

    A  helper without an endpoint: the request never leaves the process
       expected FAIL, observed FAIL
       test result: FAILED. 0 passed; 1 failed; finished in 10.05s
       panicked at persistence_tests.rs:1990  (wait_until_in_flight timed out)
    B  barrier wait removed, held connection still in place
       expected FAIL, observed PASS  <-- informative, see below
    C  the same 300 ms yield with the barrier in place
       expected PASS, observed PASS (0.41s)
    D  the manual title written back to disk before the checkpoint
       expected FAIL, observed FAIL
       panicked at persistence_tests.rs:1905  (the precondition assert)
    B' old shape: no endpoint, no barrier, 300 ms yield
       expected FAIL, observed FAIL
       test result: FAILED. 0 passed; 1 failed; finished in 8.39s
       "in-flight GeneratedTitle after unpin never adopted"

B was written expecting a failure and did not produce one, and the reason is the substance of the
fix: dropping the `wait_until_in_flight()` call does not reopen the race, because the request is
still being held open by the endpoint. The held connection is the enforcer; the wait is the
detector, pinned by A, which fails the test if the generator never dials the endpoint at all.
Without that detector a future refactor could quietly stop opening a socket and the test would pass
while proving nothing. B' is the faithful old shape and reproduces the CI failure exactly, message
and duration included; C is the same injected delay with the barrier in place and must pass, which
is the difference between an enforced ordering and a lucky one.

== 11. Repetition stability of the rewritten test

    clean tree, first run: PASS :: 1 passed; 0 failed; finished in 0.11s
    idle repeats: 150/150 passed
    repeats concurrent with the full lib suite at --test-threads=16: 80/80 passed

All source files touched by the mutations were restored by the scripts and re-compared byte for
byte at the end of every run.

== 12. Still open, on purpose

- The storage-level lost update itself: a `GeneratedTitle` that arrives before the blank is refused
  and never retried. Recovered in the product by the re-arm in `reset_session_title_to_auto`; open
  as a TODO row because "recovered by a second request" is not the same as "cannot be lost".
- The Windows leg has not produced a verdict on this batch. Two consecutive pushes were blocked
  before it: `37110356858` by this flake and `37114697856` by the guard's own colour blindness. The
  three runs before those did run it (macOS green, Windows red), and the previous batch fixed the
  five clusters those runs reported.

== 13. The gate sweep, and the tool the sweep needed first

The batch began with the usual throwaway: a scratch loop over `scripts/ci/*.py` that prints
PASS/FAIL per gate. It gave a correct verdict, 23 of 23 green, and then turned out to be
uncitable, because the script it came from is deleted with the session while the table it printed
is quoted forever. Section 14 is what closed that gap, and the sweep below is that committed
script's own output on the finished tree.

    $ scripts/verify-gates.sh
    == gates extracted from scripts/verify-in-docker.sh
       host run: the ${bootstrap} prefix is dropped, build gates are skipped
    PASS  toolchain matches the pin
    PASS  pinned protoc launcher
    PASS  cargo fmt
    PASS  ignored-test baseline
    PASS  brand/protocol guard
    PASS  protocol mirror coverage
    PASS  TODO status doc
    PASS  evidence paths in docs
    PASS  docs path references
    PASS  CI guard wiring
    PASS  host gate runner self-test
    PASS  workflow shells
    PASS  workflow toolchain
    PASS  workflow yaml
    PASS  script portability
    PASS  panic-site census
    PASS  cwd-change census
    PASS  spawn-cwd portability
    PASS  load-bearing features
    PASS  version lockstep
    PASS  installer guards
    PASS  npm package guards
    PASS  docs localization
    PASS  localization guard self-tests
    PASS  secret scan
    SKIP  cargo check (build gate; --with-build runs it)
    SKIP  cargo clippy (build gate; --with-build runs it)
    SKIP  cargo test (build gate; --with-build runs it)
    SKIP  GUI protocol types (build gate; --with-build runs it)

    all gates passed on the host (25 run, 4 skipped)

The list is not the 23 lines the scratch loop produced. It adds `toolchain matches the pin`,
`pinned protoc launcher`, `version lockstep`, `installer guards`, `npm package guards` and
`localization guard self-tests`, which the loop had quietly never run, plus `host gate runner
self-test`, new with this batch; in exchange the loop's own `bash -n scripts/verify-in-docker.sh`
line is gone, because that check now lives inside the self-test rather than beside it.
`load-bearing features` runs both directions in one gate: the 30 self-test fixtures, then the real
`cargo tree` check against the committed table. `TODO status doc` is the classifier lockstep, which is what
forces `docs/architecture/todo-open-item-classification.md` to move with `TODO.md`; this batch adds
one unchecked row (the storage-layer title gap of section 12), so the maintenance group goes from
3/17 to 4/17 and the total from 139 rows to 140.

== 14. scripts/verify-gates.sh, the runner the sweep now cites

The host-side runner reads the `gates=()` array out of `scripts/verify-in-docker.sh` rather than
keeping its own list, because a copied list is a second source of truth that stops agreeing: the
scratch loops of previous sessions had already drifted (this one covers `version lockstep`,
`installer guards`, `npm package guards`, `localization guard self-tests`,
`toolchain matches the pin` and `pinned protoc launcher`, none of which the loop had). It applies
exactly two transformations to each entry and prints both in its header: the `${bootstrap}` prefix
is dropped (the container needs it for a bind-mounted tree owned by a foreign uid; a test runner
rewriting a contributor's global git config is not acceptable), and the four build gates are
skipped unless `--with-build`. Everything else runs verbatim, including the `rustup target add`
inside the load-bearing-feature guard, which is what makes that guard fail rather than quietly skip
a target it cannot resolve.

The runner has a fixture suite of its own, `--self-test`, and that suite is itself an entry in the
array it reads, so the container and any host run execute it. 19 cases, and the ones worth their
keep are the ones about the runner's own blind spots:

    a source with no gates array is an error         (exit 2, not an empty pass)
    an entry the extractor cannot split is an error  (names the entry, does not skip it)
    a build gate is skipped by default / runs under --with-build
    the gate source is itself valid bash             (bash -n on verify-in-docker.sh)
    the real entry file still parses                 (29 labels extracted)
    a failing gate is named, its output is shown, and the exit code moves

The last two are what make the runner's own verdict mean something: if the entry script stops
parsing, or its array stops being readable, the runner says so with a non-zero exit instead of
reporting an empty pass.

Nine mutations, each restored and re-compared byte for byte. Every one is noticed by at least one
case, and the case list is what makes the suite non-vacuous. M1 to M8 mutate the runner; M9 appends
a line with an unclosed `if` to the real `scripts/verify-in-docker.sh` and must turn exactly the
syntax case red:

    M1 strip removed                  a passing set exits 0; the bootstrap placeholder is stripped
    M2 build gates never skipped      a build gate is skipped by default
    M3 the SKIP line made silent      and says that it skipped
    M4 empty extraction treated as OK a source with no gates array is an error
    M5 unsplittable entry ignored     most cases (the fixture entries stop being recognised)
    M6 `gates+=` anchor pinned to col 0  the appended gates+=(...) entry is extracted
    M7 failing gate output swallowed  the failing gate's own output is shown
    M8 exit code pinned to 0          a failing gate exits 1
    M9 gate source given bad syntax   the gate source is itself valid bash

Two of those eight were bugs in the fixtures rather than in the runner, and both survived until the
mutation was run, which is the only reason they are known. M1 escaped the first version of the
fixture entirely: the entry was `${bootstrap}; true`, and with the strip removed bash expands the
unset variable to nothing and executes the empty command followed by `true`, so the gate passed for
the wrong reason (measured: exit 0). The fixture now exports `bootstrap='exit 44'`, which a
surviving prefix expands into and aborts the gate. M6 was the extractor requiring `gates+=("...")`
at column 0, so the two full-mode entries of the real file, which are indented inside
`if [ "${MODE}" = "full" ]`, were invisible; the fixture entry was moved to the same indented shape
so that the case now fails if the anchor regresses.

Scope, stated plainly: this runner is a convenience wrapper over gates that already exist. It adds
no new check, changes no verdict, and is not a substitute for `scripts/verify-in-docker.sh`, which
still owns the claim that a cold clone works. What it removes is the per-session scratch script and
the uncitable tables those scripts produced.

== 15. The crate the batch touches, and one environment trap worth writing down

    $ cargo fmt --all -- --check                                        exit 0
    $ cargo clippy -p xai-grok-shell --all-targets -- -D warnings       exit 0
    $ cargo test -p xai-grok-shell --lib                                exit 101
    $ RUST_MIN_STACK=16777216 cargo test -p xai-grok-shell --lib
    test result: ok. 6857 passed; 0 failed; 32 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 50.36s

The third line is not a regression and it is worth recording because it looks exactly like one: the
run died on

    thread 'session::acp_session::cancel_running_task_tests::handle_prompt_frames_interrupt_on_user_message'
    has overflowed its stack
    fatal runtime error: stack overflow, aborting

after 3134 tests had already printed `ok`, which means there is no `test result:` line and no
`failures:` block to read -- the only clue is the abort line itself. This is the same
current-thread actor stack overflow that CI pins with `RUST_MIN_STACK=16777216`; the plain command
above omits it. Recorded here so the next person reads it as a missing environment variable rather
than as a broken batch.
